  "query_preview": ".. (Wednesday through Sunday)\n\nThe synoptic regime undergoes a notable transition by mid-week\nas a strong  northern-stream trough pivots across south-central\nCanada, forcing a weakly driven cold front south toward the \nUpper Midwest. By Wednesday afternoon, the frontal boundary is \nprojected to lay out east-to-west near the southern Wisconsin \nand eastern Iowa border. South-southwesterly return flow ahead \nof the boundary will tap into a plume of Gulf moisture, driving \na rebound in both heat and humidity across the Mid-Mississippi \nValley. Expect highs to reach the mid-to-upper 80s Wednesday \nafternoon, while surface dewpoints pool into the low-to-mid 70s,\nyielding heat indices pushing into the lower 90s.\n\nThe main forecast challenge and potential impact period arrives\nlate Wednesday  night into Thursday. Additional shortwave \nenergy digging across the Corn Belt will reinvigorate the \nsurface front, providing the necessary synoptic ascent to drive \nthe boundary through central Illinois during the day Thursday. \nWidespread showers and thunderstorms are expected to accompany \nthe frontal passage. Model soundings continue to highlight a \nparameter space favorable for organized convection, featuring \nsurface-based CAPE values of 1500-2500 J/kg and 0-6 km bulk \nshear on the order of 20-30 knots along the frontal zone. A \nmarginal threat for severe weather (primarily isolated damaging \nwind gusts and hail) appears plausible during the peak heating \nhours on Thursday, and this threat will continue to be\nmonitored as high-resolution guidance comes into range.\n\nGuidance continues to demonstrate some disparity regarding post-\nfrontal timing  and boundary orientation heading into Friday: \n\n1. Fast/Progressive Solution: One ensemble subset pushes the\ncold front entirely  south of the KILX CWA by Thursday evening,\nallowing a secondary bubble of weak surface high pressure to \nbuild in. This scenario would favor drier conditions for Friday \nand Saturday.\n\n2. Hung-Up Wave Solution: An alternate camp keeps the surface \nboundary stalled or draped across southern/central Illinois into\nFriday. A trailing shortwave migrating along the boundary would\nmaintain shower and thunderstorm chances through Friday \nafternoon before finally pushing the baroclinic zone south.\n\nRegardless of late-week timing nuances, global ensembles show\ngeneral consensus  on upper-level ridging building back across \nthe Midwest by next weekend. This should support a return to \nmostly dry conditions for most of the area, with temperatures \ntrending back toward the 90F mark to kick off the second week of\nAugust.",
